                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "f6857236-f2c8-4461-8d7d-2103fccabfd8",
                        "source_entry_id": "48a48b40-8346-3ada-87e7-adc179b22520",
                        "source_sense_id": "bb61dab2-1043-34f7-ad58-e6054f14ab3d",
                        "target_lang_word": "batology",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "batology",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1170c8a2-faf3-30e1-abf1-1a96b2fc7ead",
                                        "entry_id": "5194e43b-874f-3a95-a15d-3036bd4f6979",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The scientific study of plants in the genus Rubus, commonly known as brambles."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3cc1a390-63ef-442f-9ba7-eb7a4efe968f",
                                                "sense_id": "1170c8a2-faf3-30e1-abf1-1a96b2fc7ead",
                                                "text": "[…]more than another that has always been clearly recognized by British botanists, it is R. leucostachys, in spite of the various forms, which, before they were detected as hybrids, were often a puzzle to our predecessors in British batology."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3946877d-cb90-4c03-a5a9-53f2c62eb6c9",
                                                "sense_id": "1170c8a2-faf3-30e1-abf1-1a96b2fc7ead",
                                                "text": "Further knowledge of Rubus plant geography is steadily emerging, particular[ly] as co-workers such as D. Grant and T. Schofield are also contributing substantially to Yorkshire batology."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b428095d-c6ca-4374-9d2c-ad3f3a1a401b",
                                                "sense_id": "1170c8a2-faf3-30e1-abf1-1a96b2fc7ead",
                                                "text": "In Species Plantarum, in 1753, [Carl] Linnaeus identified two European species of Rubus within the large five-petaled family^([sic]) Rosa, thus beginning one of taxonomy’s largest studies—batology."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0066bb7a-28d7-40bf-8c5e-ed175ea0a1ea",
                                                "sense_id": "1170c8a2-faf3-30e1-abf1-1a96b2fc7ead",
                                                "text": "There seems to be a term for just about everything… it turns out there’s even a term for the scientific study of members of the Rubus genus— batology. No, not the study of bats. In exploring batology you’ll find that the most commonly cultivated brambles come from a complex lineage…"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "batology",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c606b8ea-c94c-3816-9bbc-51fb6211d6e3",
                                        "entry_id": "fc4fffc4-58b3-3443-b7d3-f40d4c339dbe",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The study of bats (the flying mammal)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "colloquial",
                                            "humorous",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c0a38f44-e8b7-45be-9bd9-63f136e6845d",
                                                "sense_id": "c606b8ea-c94c-3816-9bbc-51fb6211d6e3",
                                                "text": "Batology is the study of bats, while a batologist is someone who studies or collects bats as a hobby."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "a1815d96-1ca8-4e8d-bfa8-b7684fca4377",
                        "source_entry_id": "48a48b40-8346-3ada-87e7-adc179b22520",
                        "source_sense_id": "bb61dab2-1043-34f7-ad58-e6054f14ab3d",
                        "target_lang_word": "batologie",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "25ea6bbf-c462-4b97-8fbb-224d9b14cdba",
                        "source_entry_id": "48a48b40-8346-3ada-87e7-adc179b22520",
                        "source_sense_id": "d6279f34-c6b6-338b-b07b-7afc262098f0",
                        "target_lang_word": "battology",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "battology",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2b05c740-77ae-3f07-8ccd-ad4643faceab",
                                        "entry_id": "ac2cc98f-2df5-35dc-9926-7f03ff19d9b9",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Continual unnecessary reiteration of the same words, phrases, or ideas."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "879cc9c4-b012-4c34-903a-6c1f66dfad77",
                                                "sense_id": "2b05c740-77ae-3f07-8ccd-ad4643faceab",
                                                "text": "Logomachy, battology, jingling of words, are all these fine speeches!"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fbffb7f3-b2b1-4440-a887-ff015e522e13",
                                                "sense_id": "2b05c740-77ae-3f07-8ccd-ad4643faceab",
                                                "text": "This sonorous balance of phrase and epithet cannot always escape what Milton himself calls \"the heathenish battology of multiplying words.\""
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "de4d9da6-1532-49f4-b966-931fb0d5783c",
                                                "sense_id": "2b05c740-77ae-3f07-8ccd-ad4643faceab",
                                                "text": "For Watt's sense of chronology was strong, in a way, and his dislike of battology was very strong."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5114a91f-9042-475d-8974-3bc0c064d63c",
                                                "sense_id": "2b05c740-77ae-3f07-8ccd-ad4643faceab",
                                                "text": "Thus he objected to the battology of the Nicene Creed, 'God from God, Light from Light, true God from true God,' because, he said, it 'adds neither to the emphasis nor the expressiveness of the document.'"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4d5d8511-4321-4022-9012-e1edcaf6c4bd",
                                                "sense_id": "2b05c740-77ae-3f07-8ccd-ad4643faceab",
                                                "text": "“Battology” means pointlessly repeating the same thing over and over again. “Battology” means pointlessly repeating the same thing over and over again."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "6e689386-bcb9-490c-a421-6e18df5ded68",
                        "source_entry_id": "48a48b40-8346-3ada-87e7-adc179b22520",
                        "source_sense_id": "d6279f34-c6b6-338b-b07b-7afc262098f0",
                        "target_lang_word": "battologie",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "battologie",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "cb963ad4-6434-337b-af47-854150175e5b",
                                        "entry_id": "13d4bc00-385c-391a-9836-912a43daab54",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Répétition inutile d’une même chose."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2f58f3cd-9f0e-4606-8892-f2d478c6cce3",
                                                "sense_id": "cb963ad4-6434-337b-af47-854150175e5b",
                                                "text": "On se préoccupe peu de distinguer le pléonasme (admis en français) de la battologie oiseuse."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ec6a7b2f-8445-4f0f-a2dc-c8db9a50a3c8",
                                                "sense_id": "cb963ad4-6434-337b-af47-854150175e5b",
                                                "text": "C’est une battologie d’abuser de l’adverbe très : C’est très très très intéressant…"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
